# The toolchain, in a container, because this laptop stopped being able to run it.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Windows Application Control started blocking the unsigned libpq DLL that
# `psycopg[binary]` ships, so on the host every command that touches PostgreSQL dies with:
#
#     ImportError: no pq wrapper available.
#     - couldn't import psycopg 'binary' implementation: DLL load failed while importing pq:
#       Una directiva de Control de aplicaciones bloqueo este archivo.
#
# That is alembic AND pytest AND the whole backend, all at once, and it is not something a
# code change can fix: it is a machine policy. The alternative was to ask somebody to turn
# off a security feature, which is a bad trade for a convenience.
#
# So the tools run where the application already runs. Two things fall out of that, and both
# are worth saying in class:
#
#   · The tests now run against the same Python, the same libraries and the same operating
#     system as production. That was always the better answer; the DLL just forced it.
#   · The old trap disappears. Running `alembic upgrade` from the host moved the database
#     ahead of the image baked into the container, and the shop then refused to start with
#     "Can't locate revision". Same place, same code, no gap.
#
# It is NOT the image that gets deployed: that one is ../backend/Dockerfile, it has no
# pytest in it, and it should stay that way.
#
#     docker compose --profile tools run --rm tools pytest
#     docker compose --profile tools run --rm tools alembic upgrade head

FROM python:3.12-slim

WORKDIR /src

# Dev requirements, which pull in the production ones with `-r requirements.txt`.
COPY requirements.txt requirements-dev.txt ./
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements-dev.txt

# No COPY of the code on purpose: it arrives as a bind mount, so an edit on the host is
# visible here immediately and nothing has to be rebuilt to run the tests again.
